Evidence-based semantic judgment for this onelevel fork (chapter reducer below the root):
Act as the adjudicator for payload.jurisdiction, using the global, root, and
subglobal contexts to orient your review. Examine ALL findings and gaps from
payload.children. The source excerpts, original locations, evidence strength,
scope, conditions, and exceptions are the basis for each judgment.

When payload.aggregation is present, this is one bounded call inside the SAME
chapter fork. In input_kind=findings, examine the supplied leaf records, which may
contain only part of a large scan. In input_kind=judgments, children contain outcome
objects from earlier calls: review every decision, relation, gap and original
finding ID. Do not treat their summaries as independent source evidence.
For mode=final, explicitly check conditions, exceptions and contradictions ACROSS
the earlier batches, then produce one coherent chapter outcome. Carry unresolved
alternatives and their provenance forward. Every supplied finding ID must still
appear in a decision; batching never authorizes dropping evidence.

Populate decisions, relations, and gaps. Every input finding ID must appear in at
least one decision, including uncertain or contradictory findings. Group findings
only when their requirements, scope, and conditions can be faithfully judged
together. Preserve exact values, identifiers, constraints, and exceptions in the
decision statements. Give a concise justification tied to the supplied evidence.

Check support, contradiction, qualification, and exception across chunks. Explain
whether apparently conflicting claims apply to the same actors, conditions, and
scope before resolving them. Resolve a conflict only when the evidence supports
the resolution; otherwise retain both positions and their IDs as unresolved.
Never use repetition or majority voting to override a scoped exception. Context
hypotheses and previous design proposals are not independent source evidence.

Carry forward child gaps and escalate missing evidence, unresolved conflicts, and
questions outside this jurisdiction to the root. Do not invent findings for an
empty section: use empty decisions/relations arrays and explain its gaps.

